  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nRidging conditions are still expected over the area as high pressure \nremains over the South Texas, continuing the seasonally-warm \ntemperatures through Thursday. With higher PWAT values of 2+ inches, \nthere is a slight chance for scattered showers this afternoon along \nthe coast and into the Victoria Crossroads. Relatively drier air is \nfiltering into the western Gulf Thursday behind that high pressure \nwith light southeasterly winds across the CWA, keeping the region \nwarm.\n\nTemperatures will start to cool off Friday as a cold front will be \nmaking its way into South Texas, allowing for a slight 20-30% chance \nof scattered showers more inland before the front, with at least a \n60% chance along the coastline and offshore. Winds will pick up \nFriday morning and will remain out of northeast with 20-25 knots \nwell into Friday evening for the Coastal Bend, but will lighten up \nfor the Brush Country. Highs this weekend will be around 10-20 \ndegrees cooler and lows about 10-15 degrees cooler, with light winds \nthrough Saturday and Sunday. Unfortunately, these conditons will not \nlast for too long as temperatures are increasing early into next \nweek.\n\nThe frontal passage this weekend will result in gale conditions \nthrough saturday night. a gale watch will likely be needed tonight \ninto early tomorrow morning. these increased winds and seas will \nincrease the wave energy during that time period, potentially \nleading to minor coastal flooding. though confidence in minor \ncoastal flooding is low, the increase surf will likely lead to not \nideal driving conditions on the beach. accordingly, rip current risk \nwill also be elevated.",
